// `quote` covers RFC 8259's mandatory escapes: `"` and `\` explicitly and
// every character below `0x20`. FIVE of those have a named short escape, not
// two: `quote` names `\n`, `\r`, `\t`, `\b` and `\f`, and `std/json/lib.nova`
// says so at that function, a runtime builtin since 2026-10-03. This fixture
// exercises two of them, newline and tab.
//
// The other three are covered in the same ENCODE direction by
// `json_parse_strings.nova`, whose `report` prints `stringify(parse(src))`:
// its escaped-backspace, escaped-form-feed and escaped-carriage-return cases,
// plus the all-eight-together one, pin `\b`, `\f` and `\r` coming back out
// against goldens. So no coverage gap sits behind this fixture's two. This
// header claimed two named escapes because it was written in the task where
// those three arms genuinely had no coverage yet.
//
// `0x01` stands in for a control character
// with no short escape, which must render as `\u0001`. Everything at or
// above `0x20` passes through unescaped, covered by `hello` in the sibling
// fixture.
//
// The last five lines pin `quote`'s fast path, which returns a string with
// nothing to escape without rebuilding it character by character:
// - `0x1f`, the highest character below `0x20`, must still escape;
// - space and `~` must still pass through;
// - an escapable character first, or last behind a clean run, must still
//   send the whole string through the escaping loop.
fn main() {
    println(stringify(String("say \"hi\"")))
    println(stringify(String("back\\slash")))
    println(stringify(String("line\none")))
    println(stringify(String("tab\there")))
    let cs = "\u{0001}".chars()
    println(stringify(String("${cs[0]}")))
    let us = "\u{001f}".chars()
    println(stringify(String("${us[0]}")))
    println(stringify(String("a b~")))
    println(stringify(String("abcdefghij\"")))
    println(stringify(String("\"abcdefghij")))
    println(stringify(String("abcdefghij\\")))
}
